<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ismail III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1773">1773</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Abu Torab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ismail III was a young Safavid prince, Abu Torab, set on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1750 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mardan-khan-bakhtiari">Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> to lend their rule legitimacy. He held no real power: Karim Khan, who ruled as regent (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">vakil</a>), kept him in confinement, and Ismail III was the last nominal Safavid shah until his death in 1773.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-iii.jpg" alt="Ismail III" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American Numismatic Society · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_minted_in_the_name_of_Ismail_III_in_Mazandaran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
